-- title:
--   A Vitali family of cubes, and the indexing of the tiling
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with Lebesgue measure. Write $\omega_d = \lambda_d\big(\bar B(0,1)\big)$ for the volume of the closed unit ball. The module proves the scaling law
--
--   $$\lambda_d\big(\bar B(x, r)\big) \;=\; r^{d}\,\omega_d \qquad (r \ge 0,\ x \in \mathbb{R}^d),$$
--
--   fixes the constant
--
--   $$c_d \;=\; 3^{d} \;+\; \big(3\sqrt{d}\big)^{d}\,\omega_d,$$
--
--   and shows that for every $x$ the doubling inequality $\lambda_d(\bar B(x,3r)) \le c_d\,\lambda_d(\bar B(x,r))$ holds frequently as $r \downarrow 0$ — in fact for every $r > 0$. That is exactly the hypothesis of Mathlib's construction of a Vitali family, and the resulting **Vitali family of cubes** is the family in which the closed cubes live.
--
--   The module also fixes the combinatorics of the tiling at mesh $h$. The **step index** sends a point $s$ to the lattice point of $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ whose half-closed cube contains it,
--
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; \big(\mathrm{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\big)_{i},$$
--
--   and the **lattice point** map sends an integer vector $n \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ to $h\,n = (n_i h)_i$; the latter lands in $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ and is injective whenever $h \ne 0$.
--
--   The purpose of the family is a differentiation theorem along cubes that are *not* centred at the point being differentiated: a cube of side $h$ containing $s$ sits inside the ball $\bar B(s, h\sqrt d)$ and has volume $h^d$, a fixed fraction of that ball's volume, so the cubes form a Vitali family and the general averaging theorem applies. Indexing the tiles by $\mathbb{Z}^d$ rather than by the lattice as a subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$ keeps the index type countable on the nose, which is what makes a sum over tiles manipulable.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Volumes are `ℝ≥0∞`-valued and the doubling constant lives in `ℝ≥0`; the doubling statement is phrased with the "frequently in the punctured right neighbourhood of $0$" filter that the Vitali construction expects, even though the proof supplies it eventually. The scaling law for ball volume is stated for $r \ge 0$, including the degenerate $r = 0$. The step index uses `round`, whose tie-breaking convention makes the choice of tile on a boundary a fixed but arbitrary one; it is only ever used up to null sets.
-- source:
